# Written by the tool and by the runtime guards, per machine and per session:
# hook memory, rebuilt retrieval artifacts, advisory locks. None of it is
# knowledge, and committing it makes every session dirty the tree.
current/
backups/

# The advisory lock is a file, not a directory. `*.lock/` matched nothing and
# left every lock a session took sitting in `git status`.
*.lock
*.lock.stale.*
*.tmp

# Which flow this checkout is bound to is a property of this machine, not of the
# project. It is also the one file two branches always both write, so tracking
# it made an ordinary merge conflict on work that had nothing to do with it —
# and the resolution dropped a flow's whole handoff to a single line, with a
# destructive command as the only remedy offered. The records themselves are
# shared state and stay tracked.
flows/current
